# Writing Guideline

A general guide for writing product and marketing copy. Use it across projects.

---

# Core Principle

Write to bring value to the reader, not to describe what you built.

Every sentence should answer one of these questions:

- What is it?
- Why does it matter to me?
- What do I do next?

If a sentence does not move the reader closer to one of those answers, cut it.

---

# Voice

## Confident, not loud

State facts. Do not oversell them.

A confident voice trusts the reader to recognize value without hype.

Avoid words that try to force excitement instead of earning it.

---

## Warm, not casual

Friendly enough to feel human. Professional enough to be trusted.

The reader is:
- A busy adult
- Not a buddy

Avoid:
- Slang
- Emojis in body copy
- Product jokes

---

## Specific, not vague

Replace adjectives with proof.

Weak:
- `"Fast"`

Strong:
- `"Under 2 seconds"`

Use:
- Numbers
- Names
- Concrete examples

These always beat broad claims.

---

## Active, not passive

The subject should perform the action.

Strong:
- `"We send your money"`

Weak:
- `"Your money is sent"`

Active voice is:
- Shorter
- Clearer
- More honest

---

# Tone

Tone changes with context. Voice stays consistent.

| Context | Tone |
|---|---|
| Hero and marketing | Aspirational, energetic |
| Product and feature copy | Clear, practical |
| Errors and warnings | Calm, helpful |
| Success and confirmation | Brief, reassuring |
| Legal and policy | Plain, complete |
| Empty states | Encouraging, directional |

When in doubt:
- Choose clarity over cleverness

---

# Sentence Length

- Keep sentences short
- Most should stay under `20 words`

Read every sentence aloud.

If you run out of breath:
- Split it in half

---

## Rhythm

Vary sentence rhythm intentionally.

- Three short sentences in a row feels choppy
- One longer sentence after two short ones feels deliberate

Use:
- Long sentences to explain
- Short sentences to land the point

---

## One Idea Per Sentence

If you use `"and"` twice:
- You probably have two sentences hiding inside one

---

# Word Choice

## Use Simple Words

Prefer the shorter word when meaning stays the same.

Better:
- `use` instead of `utilize`
- `help` instead of `facilitate`
- `show` instead of `demonstrate`

Smart writing sounds simple.

---

## Cut Filler

Most adverbs add no value.

Common filler:
- very
- really
- simply
- easily
- basically
- actually
- literally
- just

Delete them whenever possible.

---

## Avoid Empty Words

These sound impressive but communicate nothing:

- revolutionary
- game changing
- world class
- cutting edge
- next generation
- best in class
- holistic
- synergy
- leverage
- robust
- seamless

If removing the word changes nothing:
- Remove it

---

## Use Second Person

Use:
- `"you"`
- `"your"`

Avoid:
- `"users"`
- `"customers"`

Write to one person at a time.

---

## Verbs Over Nouns

Strong:
- `"We help you decide"`

Weak:
- `"We provide decision support"`

Verbs create movement.

---

# Headlines

A strong headline should do one of these:

- State a desired outcome
- Name a real problem
- Make a specific promise

A headline should not describe the product itself.

---

## Examples

Weak:
- `"A platform for modern teams"`

Strong:
- `"Ship faster without breaking things"`

---

Weak:
- `"Innovative payment solutions"`

Strong:
- `"Get paid in 12 currencies, settle the same day"`

---

## Headline Rules

- Aim for `6–12 words`
- Maximum two lines
- Remove every unnecessary word

---

# Subheads

The subhead expands the headline with:
- One specific detail
- One concrete proof point

Rules:
- One sentence
- Under `25 words`

Examples:
- If the headline promises speed → include the number
- If the headline promises ease → explain the steps

---

# Body Copy

## Lead With the Conclusion

Readers scan before they read.

Structure:
1. Most useful sentence first
2. Supporting explanation second

---

## Group Ideas

- One paragraph = one idea
- Start a new paragraph when the topic changes

Even short paragraphs are fine.

---

## Show, Don't Tell

Avoid:
- `"easy to use"`
- `"trusted"`

Instead:
- Explain what makes it easy
- Name who trusts it

---

# Calls to Action

Buttons are promises.

They should explain what happens next.

Rules:
- `2–4 words`
- Verb first

---

## Strong CTA Examples

- Open account
- Start free trial
- Talk to sales
- See pricing

---

## Weak CTA Examples

- Learn more
- Click here
- Submit
- Get started

If the action is unclear:
- Rewrite it

---

## First Person CTA

Use first person when it fits:

- `"Start my trial"`

Often feels more committed than:

- `"Start your trial"`

Test what fits the brand voice.

---

# Microcopy

Small text often matters more than large text.

It shapes the product experience directly.

---

# Form Labels

Keep labels:
- Short
- Clear

Strong:
- `"Work email"`

Weak:
- `"Please enter your work email address"`

---

## Help Text

- Place below the field
- Never inside the input

---

# Errors

Error messages should be:
- Calm
- Useful
- Actionable

Strong:
- `"We couldn't find that account. Check the email and try again."`

Weak:
- `"Invalid input"`

Never blame the reader.

---

# Empty States

A blank screen should teach the user:
- What belongs here
- Why it is empty
- How to fill it

Usually:
- One sentence is enough

---

# Success Messages

Keep them brief.

Strong:
- `"Saved"`

Weak:
- `"Your changes have been successfully saved to your account"`

The action already communicates success.

---

# Tooltips

- One sentence maximum
- Define the term
- Explain the choice

Do not:
- Sell
- Repeat the label

---

# Numbers and Proof

Numbers build trust.

Use them whenever possible:
- Countries
- Currencies
- Customers
- Years
- Percentages
- Time saved

---

## Specific Numbers Feel Real

Weak:
- `"Saves about 4 hours a week"`

Strong:
- `"Saves 4.2 hours a week"`

Specificity increases credibility.

---

## Never Invent Numbers

If data is missing:
- Write `[NEEDS DATA]`
- Ask for the source

Fake stats destroy trust quickly.

---

# Capitalization

Default:
- Sentence case

Use sentence case for:
- Headings
- Body copy
- Microcopy

It feels more natural and less corporate.

---

## Title Case

Allowed for:
- Hero headlines
- Major section headers

Only if the brand requires a more dramatic tone.

Pick one approach per project and stay consistent.

---

## ALL CAPS

Allowed only for:
- Tiny labels
- Eyebrow text
- Badges

Never use ALL CAPS for full sentences.

---

# Punctuation

- Use periods for full sentences
- Skip periods on:
  - Headlines
  - Buttons
  - Navigation
  - Short labels

---

## Commas

Use commas to separate ideas.

Do not use commas to artificially stretch sentences.

If a comma behaves like a period:
- Use the period instead

---

## Exclamation Marks

Avoid them.

Rules:
- Maximum one per page
- Only when genuinely deserved

Too many feel forced.

---

## Em Dashes

Avoid em dashes when:
- A period works
- A comma works

Too many em dashes make writing feel breathless.

---

# Inclusive Writing

Write so anyone can understand it.

Avoid:
- Jargon
- Idioms
- Culture-specific references

Use gender-neutral language by default.

`They` is acceptable for unknown people.

---

## Acronyms

Always define acronyms on first use.

Never assume:
- Internal terminology is obvious

---

# What to Avoid

## Empty Hype

Avoid:
- revolutionary
- world class
- cutting edge

---

## Filler Phrases

Avoid:
- at the end of the day
- in today's world
- more than ever before

---

## Hedging

Avoid:
- we believe
- we think
- we hope to

Be direct.

---

## Unnecessary Apologies

Avoid:
- unfortunately
- we're sorry to inform you

Use calm, factual language instead.

---

## Selling Inside the Product

Once the user is inside the product:
- Stop pitching
- Help them complete the task

---

# Line Endings

All text blocks:
- Headings
- Titles
- Descriptions
- Body copy

Must never end with:
- A single-word last line
- A two-word last line

Rewrite until the final line contains at least:
- `3–4 words`

---

## Titles and Headings

Titles should:
- Fit on one line at target font size
- Be verified at desktop width before finalizing

---

# Self Edit Checklist

Before shipping any sentence, ask:

- Would the reader understand this in five seconds?
- Did I use a number, name, or concrete example?
- Did I remove every unnecessary adverb?
- Is the verb active?
- Could a competitor paste this onto their site?
  - If yes, make it more specific
- Does it sound human when read aloud?

If the sentence fails any check:
- Rewrite it